<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/00TG">Lemma <span data-tag="00TG">3.1.2.10</span>.</a> For every pair of integers $0 &lt; i \leq n$, the horn inclusion $f_0: \Lambda ^{n}_{i} \hookrightarrow \Delta ^ n$ is a retract of the inclusion map $f: (\Delta ^1 \times \Lambda ^ n_ i) \coprod _{ \{ 1\}  \times \Lambda ^{n}_ i} ( \{ 1\}  \times \Delta ^ n) \hookrightarrow \Delta ^1 \times \Delta ^ n$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
<p>
<strong>Proof.</strong>
      Let $A_{}$ denote the simplicial subset of $\Delta ^1 \times \Delta ^ n$ given by the union of $\Delta ^1 \times \Lambda ^ n_ i$ with $\{  1 \}  \times \Delta ^ n$. To prove Lemma <a data-tag="00TG" href="/tag/00TG">3.1.2.10</a>, it will suffice to show that there exists a commutative diagram of simplicial sets 
    <p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ \{ 0\}  \times \Lambda ^{n}_{i} \ar [r] \ar [d]^{f_0} &amp;  A_{} \ar [r] \ar [d]^{f} &amp;  \Lambda ^{n}_{i} \ar [d]^{f_0} \\ \{ 0\}  \times \Delta ^ n \ar [r] &amp;  \Delta ^1 \times \Delta ^ n \ar [r]^-{r} &amp;  \Delta ^ n } \]
</div>
<p>
       where the left horizontal maps are given by inclusion and the horizontal compositions are the identity maps. To achieve this, it suffices to choose $r$ to be given on vertices by the map of partially ordered sets 
    <p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  r: [1] \times [n] \rightarrow [n] \quad \quad r(j,k) = \begin{cases}  i &amp;  \text{ if $j=1$ and $k \leq i$ } \\  k &amp;  \text{ otherwise. } \end{cases} \]
</div>
<span class="qed">$\square$</span>
</p>
</p></p></p></article>
